  "body": "I refer to the rule stated here: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#focus-fixup-rule-one \n\nBrowsers implement this rule differently: Chrome implements this rule according to the spec. Firefox and Safari do not follow the spec, but their behavior is more intuitive and avoids a keyboard trap.\n\nExample: https://jsfiddle.net/4f9a3ucq/\n\nIn chrome, when tabbing through the menu items in the example fiddle, a keyboard trap is caused as we try to focus on an element that becomes hidden AS we are trying to focus on it (a sub-menu is visible when the parent menu item is in focus, then when tab is pressed the focus tries to move to the first sub-menu item but it becomes hidden due to “display:none” style as focus moves away from the parent).\n\nThis falls under the focus fixup rule one: 'the designated focused area of a control group is removed from that control group in some way'. The spec states that in this case 'the first non-inert focused area in that control group to be the new focused area of the control group'. In Chrome, focus is returned to the bodyElement which causes it to cycle through the same subset of elements in the page. In Safari and Firefox, focus moves onto the next available element in the page, so the user can tab through the remaining elements in the page.\n\nI would suggest changing focus fixup rule one to be more usable by moving focus to the next focusable area rather than the first focusable area (as Safari and Firefox do).\n",
